  "topic": "Smart Pointers (std::unique_ptr and std::shared_ptr)",
  "explanation": "Smart pointers in C++ are template classes that manage dynamic memory automatically, ensuring proper resource deallocation and preventing memory leaks. They are used to implement RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization) idioms, providing safer and more expressive memory management compared to raw pointers. Modern C++ (C++11 and beyond) introduced std::unique_ptr and std::shared_ptr to facilitate ownership semantics, with std::unique_ptr providing exclusive ownership and std::shared_ptr enabling shared ownership with reference counting.",
  "syntax": "template <typename T> class std::unique_ptr {\npublic:\n    explicit unique_ptr(T* ptr = nullptr) noexcept;\n    ~unique_ptr();\n    unique_ptr(const unique_ptr&) = delete;\n    unique_ptr& operator=(const unique_ptr&) = delete;\n    unique_ptr(unique_ptr&&) noexcept;\n    unique_ptr& operator=(unique_ptr&&) noexcept;\n    T* get() const noexcept;\n    T& operator*() const; \n    T* operator->() const;\n    void reset(T* ptr = nullptr); \n    T* release() noexcept;\n};\n\n// For shared ownership:\ntemplate <typename T> class std::shared_ptr {\npublic:\n    explicit shared_ptr(T* ptr = nullptr); \n    ~shared_ptr();\n    shared_ptr(const shared_ptr&);\n    shared_ptr& operator=(const shared_ptr&);\n    T* get() const noexcept;\n    T& operator*() const; \n    T* operator->() const;\n    void reset(T* ptr = nullptr); \n    long use_count() const noexcept;\n};",
  "code_example": [
    "#include <memory>",
    "#include <iostream>",
    "",
    "class Widget {",
    "public:",
    "    Widget() { std::cout << \"Widget created\" << std::endl; }",
    "    ~Widget() { std::cout << \"Widget destroyed\" << std::endl; }",
    "    void do_something() { std::cout << \"Doing something\" << std::endl; }",
    "};",
    "",
    "int main() {",
    "    // Using std::unique_ptr for exclusive ownership",
    "    std::unique_ptr<Widget> ptr1 = std::make_unique<Widget>();",
    "    ptr1->do_something();",
    "    // Transfer ownership to ptr2",
    "    std::unique_ptr<Widget> ptr2 = std::move(ptr1);",
    "    if (!ptr1) { std::cout << \"ptr1 is now null\" << std::endl; }",
    "    ptr2->do_something();",
    "",
    "    // Using std::shared_ptr for shared ownership",
    "    std::shared_ptr<Widget> sp1 = std::make_shared<Widget>();",
    "    {",
    "        std::shared_ptr<Widget> sp2 = sp1; // shared ownership",
    "        std::cout << \"use_count: \" << sp1.use_count() << std::endl;",
    "    }",
    "    std::cout << \"use_count after inner scope: \" << sp1.use_count() << std::endl;",
    "    return 0;",
    "}"
  ],
  "example_explanation": "This example demonstrates modern C++ smart pointers by creating a Widget object managed by std::unique_ptr, which ensures automatic destruction when it goes out of scope, preventing memory leaks. It also shows shared ownership with std::shared_ptr, where multiple shared pointers can own the same object, with reference counting managing the object's lifetime. The example highlights ownership transfer with std::move and reference counting with use_count(), illustrating how smart pointers facilitate safe and expressive resource management in C++.",
